  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM Monday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Some patchy dense fog is possible late tonight and \nespecially on Tuesday night.  \n\nThe upper level disturbance that produced some VFR sprinkles and \nspotty rain earlier today is now exiting the area. Areas of mid and \nhigh clouds are now shifting east. \n\nThe low level flow will gradually veer around and become more \neasterly and southeasterly supporting an increase in low level \nmoisture. As we dive deeper into the transition season with cooler \nand longer nights, the potential for fog and some stratus\nincreases. Areas of fog are possible tonight across eastern \nareas in the Coastal Plain and the eastern Piedmont and more \nwidespread and impactful on Tuesday night and Wednesday morning.\n\nOutside of the fog and stratus, fair weather is expected over the \nnext couple of days with highs in the mid and upper 70s on Tuesday \nwith lows tonight and Tuesday night in the 40s to around 50.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures \ncontinue through Friday with rain chances shifting primarily to \nFriday afternoon, followed by a dry, cooler weekend. \n\nA frontal system approaching the Mississippi Valley Wednesday \nmorning will lift a warm front northward across the Mid-Atlantic on \nThursday ahead of an approaching cold front. The primary cold front \nwill cross the region Thursday night into Friday before pushing \noffshore Friday evening, followed by a weak, dry reinforcing \nboundary early Saturday.\n\nAs mentioned before, model guidance has trended notably drier for \nThursday, shifting the primary focus for precipitation to Friday \nafternoon. The highest coverage remains focused along and east of \nthe US1 corridor. PoPs and QPF have been trimmed back for Thursday, \nthough an isolated shower or thunderstorm remains possible during \nthe afternoon. The good news is that models continue to agree and \nshow the reinforcing dry frontal boundary sweeping across the region \nlate Friday into early Saturday, assuring a dry weekend.\n\nTemperatures will run 10 to 15 degrees above climatological normal \nthrough Friday, with highs reaching the mid 70s to low 80s and mild \novernight lows in the mid 50s to low 60s. Expect overnight lows to \nreturn to the 40s Saturday night, with highs moderating into the 60s \nSunday and Monday.",
